// `+` on two lists is CONCATENATION, and the untyped `[]` is the interesting
// half rather than an edge case.
//
// The operator shares one AST node with arithmetic, so the builder has to
// route it to concatenation before it reaches `arith`, exactly as it does
// for `"a" + "b"`.
//
// Five shapes, and each one pins a different clause. Two typed lists is the
// ordinary case. `xs + []` and `[] + xs` are the SAME operation written two ways
// and both are legal, so whichever side carries an element type has to decide
// and the other has to be coerced — an asymmetric rule would refuse one
// spelling. `[] + []` has no element type on either side and needs none: the
// result stays untyped and the position that consumes it decides, which is what
// `empty` demonstrates by being annotated at the binding. And `List<String>`
// beside `List<Int>` is here because the lowered call is generic in the element
// type, so a single-element-type fixture could not tell a correct lowering from
// one that hard-codes Int.
//
// The chained form is the shape `std/json.shape_error_prepend` actually writes
// (`[segment] + e.path`), which is why this exists at all: it was the ONLY
// blocker in that function, and every `derive FromJson` body calls it.
import std/io

fn main() {
    a = [1, 2]
    b = [3]
    io.print(Debug.inspect(a + b))
    io.print(Debug.inspect(a + []))
    io.print(Debug.inspect([] + b))
    empty: List<Int> = [] + []
    io.print(Debug.inspect(empty))
    s = ["x"]
    io.print(Debug.inspect(["w"] + s + ["y", "z"]))
}
